# Editable Prompt System with Per-Project Override

**Keywords:** prompts, system prompt, ceremony, per-project override, prompts/*.md, role sections, WorkspaceFilesPort, resolve_role_body, compose_role_system, language directive

## Overview

The Editable Prompt System defines the role-based system prompts (PO, SM, BA, SA, PD, DEV) and composers that assemble each final system prompt from base invariants plus a per-role section. It is the mechanism by which every ceremony/task decision is framed in a consistent voice and format for the agent.

The **per-project override** capability — letting a workspace override these prompts from `<root>/prompts/*.md` — is now implemented as a resolution seam in `crates/application/src/prompts_resolve.rs` (the F001 milestone). A workspace can replace any individual role body without touching base invariants. The seam exists and is fully tested against an in-memory files double; however it is NOT yet adopted by any production dispatch call site — every live task path still uses the embedded constants directly through `system_prompt`.

## How it works

All default prompt text lives as embedded constants in `crates/application/src/prompts.rs`: one constant per ceremony role (`PO`, `SM`, `BA`, `SA`, `PD`, `DEV`) plus extended roles (`DEV_HEAL`, `TEST`, `DOCS`); shared invariants are in `BASE`, `PROCESS_INVARIANTS`, and `ENGINEERING_STANDARDS`. Two composers exist:

```rust
// crates/application/src/prompts.rs:357  — embedded-only; used by every live call site
pub fn system_prompt(role_section: &str) -> String
// -> format!("{BASE}\n\n{ENGINEERING_STANDARDS}\n\n{role_section}")

// crates/application/src/prompts_resolve.rs:90  — override-aware seam; not wired into dispatch
pub async fn compose_role_system(
    files: Option<&dyn WorkspaceFilesPort>, work_dir: &Path,
    prompts_subdir: &str, role_key: &str,
) -> String
```

Live callers select their role's prompt via a one-line selector such as:

```rust
let sp = system_prompt(prompts::BA);
```

Every decision path — dev tasks (`use_cases/run_dev*.rs`), test/docs tasks (`run_test.rs`), PO/SM/BA/SA ceremonies (`run_ba.rs`, etc.) — calls this composer with its own embedded constant.

The override path resolves a body through three functions:

1. **`load_role_override(files, work_dir, prompts_subdir, role_key)`** (prompts_resolve.rs:54) reads `<work_dir>/<prompts_subdir>/<role_key>.md` through [`WorkspaceFilesPort::read`](crates/application/src/ports/outbound/workspace.rs). It returns `None` when there is no port or file present.
2. **`resolve_role_body(embedded_body, loaded_override)`** (prompts_resolve.rs:76) makes the pure precedence decision:
   - non-blank loaded override → trimmed override wins;
   - missing / blank / whitespace-only / rejected key → embedded body wins.
3. **`compose_role_system(...)`** wraps BASE + ENGINEERING_STANDARDS around whichever body won.

Role keys are gated by an explicit whitelist so typos or stray files are never injected into a system prompt:

```rust
pub const KNOWN_ROLE_KEYS: &[&str] =
    &["po", "sm", "ba", "sa", "pd", "dev", "dev-heal", "test", "docs"];
```

Resolution deliberately keeps IO out of application code — reads go through the files port only; decisions are pure functions over what was read.

## Usage

Place optional markdown bodies under a project root's canonical sub-directory (default name below):

```text
project-root/
  prompts/
    po.md       # optional PO-body override ("po")
    sm.md       # optional SM-body override ("sm")
    ba.md       # optional BA-body override ("ba")
    sa.md       # optional SA-body override ("sa")
    pd.md       # optional PD-body override ("pd")
    dev.md      # DEV and DEV_HEAL both map to key "dev"
    test.md     # TEST-body ("test")
    docs.md     # DOCS-body ("docs")
```

Rules applied at load time:

- A missing file leaves that role on its embedded default.
- A blank or whitespace-only file counts as ABSENT → embedded default wins (no empty section leaking into every prompt).
- A non-blank file replaces ONLY that one role's body; BASE and ENGINEERING_STANDARDS always survive unchanged around it.
- A stray/non-role file such as `README.md` is rejected outright and never injected.

As of this writing these files are read *only* if something calls [`compose_role_system`] / [`load_role_override`]; no production task-dispatch path does yet (see Edge cases). Running normal ceremonies today behaves exactly as before this feature landed — all roles use their embedded text.

## Interface

### Functions/types

| Name | Location | Signature / note |
|------|----------|------------------|
| [`system_prompt(role_section)`](crates/application/src/prompts.rs) | prompts.rs | Embedded composer used by all live call sites; returns full rendered string |
| [`compose_role_system(...)`](crates/application/src/prompts_resolve.rs) | prompts_resolve | Override-aware composer; BASE + ENGINEERING_STANDARDS + resolved body |
| [`load_role_override(...)`](crates/application/src/prompts_resolve.rs) | prompts_resolve | Reads one `<root>/<subdir>/<key>.md` through the files port |
| [`resolve_role_body(embedded?, loaded?) -> Cow<'_, str>`](crates/application/src/prompts_resolve.rs) | prompts_resolve | Pure precedence decision between embedded default and loaded body |
| [`is_valid_role_key(key)`](crates/application/src/prompts_resolve.rs) | prompts_resolve | Whitelist membership check |

### Constants

- **[DEFAULT_PROMPTS_SUBDIR = "prompts"](crates/application/src/prompts_resolve.rs)** — canonical sub-directory for overrides relative to the project root.
- **[KNOWN_ROLE_KEYS](#)** = ["po","sm","ba","sa","pd","dev","dev-heal","test","docs"].
- Base/Role section constants in [prompts.rs](#): PROCESS_INVARIANTS vs ENGINEERING-STANDARDS plus one per ceremony/extended role above.

### Selector pattern at call sites

Each dispatch path names its own section explicitly:

```rust
use crate::prompts;
let sp = system_prompt(prompts::DEV);   // dev task briefings ...
let sp = system_prompt(prompts::TEST);
let sp = system_prompt(prompts::DOCS);
// ...one per PO/SM/BAA ceremony path today;
// none route through compose_role_system yet.
```

There are no endpoints or CLI flags; prompting happens entirely inside application-layer task construction.

## Configuration

Settings that change what appears inside composed content:

- **Prompt source directory** — [DEFAULT_PROMPTS_SUBDIR](#)==“prompt”（currently only reachable via tests since resolution isn’t wired to runtime）。
